Difficulty Explanation (optional)
Describe in your own words why your task is challenging for humans and agents to solve.

This task was hard because the bug was not in only one place. It was spread across many C++ replay modules and all of them were affecting the same trace output. The cache identity was becoming stale during hot reload, journal events were coming in wrong order, checkpoint seal was not matching with WAL, and generation counters were getting different values in different views. Recovery flow was also running screening again without properly finishing the swap process, and pack layer was using wrong T7 residual tolerance. Because of this, fixing only one file was not enough. Even if the output JSON looked correct, the tests were still failing because they recompute body digest, checkpoint seal, WAL checksum, and generation values from replay instead of trusting the final trace file.
Solution Explanation (optional)
Describe your high-level approach to this task and key insights in forming the solution.

I first ran the replay pipeline and compared the screen, swap, and live views with the generated trace and WAL logs to find where the generation values were getting different. I found that screening was finishing before swap was completed, some swap signatures were getting skipped, recovery was not quiescing swap before running screening again, and seal calculation was missing the required BEEF mix after every bust and screen pair. I fixed the issues across all related modules so cache refresh, journal ordering, checkpoint seal, generation counters, and pack tolerance all stayed consistent. After that I rebuilt the project, cleared the runtime data, reran all replay scenarios, and generated the trace again from the normal replay flow instead of editing anything manually.
Verification Explanation (optional)
Explain how your tests are verifying correctness.

I verified the fix by rebuilding the project, clearing the runtime directory, and running cold replay and all scenario replays again. This regenerated the trace file completely from scratch. The checks confirmed the trace schema, body digest, checkpoint seal, WAL checksum, generation values, action codes, and block RMS values were all correct. I also checked cold and warm replay results, cross-view generation matching, WAL ordering, recovery idempotence, corrupt WAL handling, replay stability, and T7 tolerance. This made sure the fix was working properly and not only for one test case.


